WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.833 --> 00:00:11.833
[electronic music]

00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:19.500
Sandeep: Hi.
My name is Sandeep,

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.300
a developer advocate
on the Google Cloud platform.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:23.933
Welcome to the Google
Data Center

00:00:23.933 --> 00:00:25.533
at the Dalles, Oregon.

00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:27.100
Take a look around.

00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:29.500
Before we go inside,
we need to make sure

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.500
that we have the appropriate
security clearance.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.933
Most Google employees can't
even get in here.

00:00:34.933 --> 00:00:39.833
So let's go on a special
behind-the-scenes tour.

00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:44.833
[keypad beeps, door opens]

00:00:44.833 --> 00:00:46.266
I'm here with Noah

00:00:46.266 --> 00:00:48.466
from the Site Reliability
Engineering Team.

00:00:48.466 --> 00:00:49.833
Noah, can you tell us
a little bit more

00:00:49.833 --> 00:00:51.533
about the SRE role at Google?

00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:53.666
Noah: Yeah, SREs write and
maintain the software systems

00:00:53.666 --> 00:00:55.633
designed to keep
our services running.

00:00:55.633 --> 00:00:57.833
Sandeep: So what happens if
one of these systems goes down?

00:00:57.833 --> 00:01:00.233
Noah: We've designed our systems
from the ground up

00:01:00.233 --> 00:01:02.200
to be able to handle
any unexpected failures

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.400
that might occur.

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:05.233
We have highly redundant
power, networking,

00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:07.600
and serving domains so that
even if we do lose

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:10.666
an entire cluster, we're able
to re-direct those workloads

00:01:10.666 --> 00:01:13.733
and live migrate data in
order to minimize any impact.

00:01:13.733 --> 00:01:17.933
In addition, we have a team
of SREs on call 24/7

00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:20.333
that can tackle any problems
that might arise.

00:01:20.333 --> 00:01:21.700
Sandeep: Thanks, Noah.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:23.133
Now we've learned more
about the systems

00:01:23.133 --> 00:01:24.766
that manage our fleet
at Google,

00:01:24.766 --> 00:01:25.900
let's take a deeper look

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:29.266
at the data center
infrastructure itself.

00:01:29.266 --> 00:01:31.000
Before we can
continue further,

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:33.166
we need to go through
the biometric iris scan

00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:34.566
and circle lock.

00:01:34.566 --> 00:01:37.033
These only allow one person
in at a time

00:01:37.033 --> 00:01:38.933
and require
dual authentication

00:01:38.933 --> 00:01:40.200
to continue further.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.800
I'll see you
on the other side.

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:45.966
[control beeps]

00:01:45.966 --> 00:01:47.266
computer voice: Please
situate your eyes

00:01:47.266 --> 00:01:52.200
to begin the procedure.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.466
Please come a little closer
to the camera.

00:01:55.466 --> 00:01:56.700
[beep]

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.200
Sandeep: Welcome to
the data center floor.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:01.900
As you can tell, we have
a lot of servers,

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:04.533
and this is a single cluster
in a single floor

00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:06.200
in a single building.

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:08.900
Managing all of these servers
on a global scale

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.200
is quite a challenge.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.500
To utilize our fleet,
we use tools

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:16.533
such as Borg, Colossus,
and Spanner.

00:02:16.533 --> 00:02:18.366
You may be familiar
with similar tools,

00:02:18.366 --> 00:02:20.666
such as Kubernetes,
Google Cloud storage,

00:02:20.666 --> 00:02:22.400
and BigQuery.

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:24.433
These tools allow
Google engineers

00:02:24.433 --> 00:02:25.800
and Cloud customers

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.766
to more easily manage
infrastructure,

00:02:27.766 --> 00:02:28.933
allowing everyone to build

00:02:28.933 --> 00:02:32.433
innovative and scalable
applications.

00:02:36.433 --> 00:02:39.900
Here at Google, a lot of our
infrastructure is custom-made.

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.100
This gives us the flexibility
and performance

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:44.800
we need to run
all of our services at scale.

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:46.500
Oh, hey, it's Virginia,
one of our network engineers.

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:47.766
Virginia: Hey, Sandeep.

00:02:47.766 --> 00:02:49.133
Sandeep: Virginia,
what are you working on today?

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:50.766
Virginia: Today I'm working
with Hardware Ops

00:02:50.766 --> 00:02:52.500
to expand
this data center network

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.933
to deploy additional machines
in this building.

00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:57.800
Our fleet is constantly growing
to support new capacity

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.000
for Google products
and our Cloud customers.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.233
Sandeep: That sounds like
a lot of work,

00:03:01.233 --> 00:03:03.433
to be constantly adding capacity
around the globe.

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:05.133
Virginia: Well, we designed
our network

00:03:05.133 --> 00:03:07.666
so that this kind of capacity
growth isn't very hard.

00:03:07.666 --> 00:03:10.766
Jupiter, our current data center
and network technology,

00:03:10.766 --> 00:03:12.500
is a hierarchical design

00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:14.733
using software-defined
networking principles.

00:03:14.733 --> 00:03:16.533
So just like with our servers,

00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:19.333
we abstracted away the specific
details of our network

00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:22.333
and can manage them like they're
software programs and data.

00:03:22.333 --> 00:03:25.166
Sandeep: Abstracting seems to be
a common theme here at Google.

00:03:25.166 --> 00:03:26.500
I've also noticed
there's a lot of fiber

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.700
running in our data centers.
Virginia: That's right.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.533
A single building can
support 75,000 machines,

00:03:31.533 --> 00:03:34.500
and carry over one petabit
per second of bandwidth,

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:37.133
which is actually more than
on the entire Internet.

00:03:37.133 --> 00:03:38.400
Sandeep: Wow.
Virginia: This allows us

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.333
to reliably access storage
and compute resources

00:03:41.333 --> 00:03:43.633
with low latency
and high throughput.

00:03:43.633 --> 00:03:45.633
Sandeep: So how is
this data center connected to

00:03:45.633 --> 00:03:47.900
all our other data centers
around the globe?

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:49.400
Virginia: Google runs B4,

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.366
our own private, highly
efficient backbone network,

00:03:52.366 --> 00:03:53.633
which is actually growing faster

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.633
than our Internet-facing
network.

00:03:55.633 --> 00:03:57.766
It connects all
our data centers together

00:03:57.766 --> 00:04:00.500
and allows services to
efficiently access resources

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:02.266
in any location.
Sandeep: Nice.

00:04:02.266 --> 00:04:04.766
I finally know what all this
Google fiber is really used for.

00:04:04.766 --> 00:04:07.700
Thanks, Virginia.
Virginia: No problem.

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:08.866
Sandeep: So now you've seen

00:04:08.866 --> 00:04:10.766
all the compute and networking
horsepower

00:04:10.766 --> 00:04:12.433
required to run your workloads
in the Cloud,

00:04:12.433 --> 00:04:14.666
let's take a look
at where your data is

00:04:14.666 --> 00:04:16.633
safely and securely stored.

00:04:16.633 --> 00:04:19.800
Let's go.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.533
Whether you're querying
terabytes of data on BigQuery

00:04:22.533 --> 00:04:25.066
or storing petabytes
in Google Cloud Storage,

00:04:25.066 --> 00:04:28.400
all of your data needs to
be stored on a physical device.

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.333
Our data center infrastructure
allows us

00:04:30.333 --> 00:04:33.966
to access our storage
quickly and securely.

00:04:33.966 --> 00:04:37.066
At our scale, we need to handle
hard drive and SSD failure

00:04:37.066 --> 00:04:38.666
on a daily basis.

00:04:38.666 --> 00:04:40.500
While your data
is replicated and safe,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.400
we need to destroy or recycle
used hard drives

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:45.866
so no one can access your data.

00:04:45.866 --> 00:04:47.933
From the time a disc
is removed from the server

00:04:47.933 --> 00:04:49.400
to the time it's decommissioned,

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:52.033
we maintain a very strict chain
of custody.

00:04:52.033 --> 00:04:54.700
The discs are completely wiped
and then destroyed

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:56.333
in a huge shredder.

00:04:56.333 --> 00:04:59.333
Let's go shred some hard drives.

00:05:06.533 --> 00:05:08.533
[beeping]

00:05:17.966 --> 00:05:19.500
We've looked at
a lot of the hardware

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.500
that runs in our data centers,
but it doesn't end there.

00:05:22.500 --> 00:05:25.133
We need to cool and power
our infrastructure

00:05:25.133 --> 00:05:28.300
in an environmentally
sustainable and reliable way.

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:31.300
Let's take a look
at how we cool our servers.

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:35.866
Welcome to the mechanical
equipment room.

00:05:35.866 --> 00:05:38.000
Looks pretty cool,
doesn't it?

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.033
Oh, hey, it's Brian,
one of

00:05:39.033 --> 00:05:40.866
our data center
facilities technicians!

00:05:40.866 --> 00:05:42.466
Brian: Hey, Sandeep.
Sandeep: Hey, Brian.

00:05:42.466 --> 00:05:44.766
Brian, can you tell us a little
bit more about this room?

00:05:44.766 --> 00:05:46.633
Brian: Sure.
This is a cooling plant

00:05:46.633 --> 00:05:49.100
for one of the data centers
that we have on site.

00:05:49.100 --> 00:05:51.933
So a lot of heat is generated
on the server floor,

00:05:51.933 --> 00:05:53.233
and it all has to be removed,

00:05:53.233 --> 00:05:55.333
and that starts right here
in the cooling plant.

00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:57.033
So it's basically two loops.

00:05:57.033 --> 00:05:58.533
We have the condenser
water loop

00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:00.266
and we have
the process water loop.

00:06:00.266 --> 00:06:03.566
The process water loop are these
blue and red pipes over here.

00:06:03.566 --> 00:06:05.466
So they take the heat
off the server floor

00:06:05.466 --> 00:06:08.400
and they transfer it to
these heat exchangers here.

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:09.500
The condenser water loop are

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:11.800
these green and yellow
pipes here.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:14.433
They take the cold water
from the basin underneath us,

00:06:14.433 --> 00:06:16.533
they transfer it to
these heat exchangers here,

00:06:16.533 --> 00:06:18.966
and they send it up to the
cooling towers up on the roof.

00:06:18.966 --> 00:06:20.866
Sandeep: I notice our pipes
are Google colors.

00:06:20.866 --> 00:06:22.100
It's pretty cool.

00:06:22.100 --> 00:06:24.500
So how efficient is
our data center?

00:06:24.500 --> 00:06:25.666
Brian: Well, Google has
some of

00:06:25.666 --> 00:06:27.800
the most efficient data centers
in the world.

00:06:27.800 --> 00:06:30.233
In fact, when we started
reporting our power usage effectiveness

00:06:30.233 --> 00:06:32.566
or P.U.E., in 2008,

00:06:32.566 --> 00:06:36.033
most data centers
were around 100% overhead.

00:06:36.033 --> 00:06:38.700
At that point in time,
Google was 20% overhead,

00:06:38.700 --> 00:06:41.733
but since then,
we've reduced it to just 12%,

00:06:41.733 --> 00:06:43.700
and that even includes
our cafeterias.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:46.500
Sandeep: Whoa!
That is so low!

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.800
Also what's this big
green machine for?

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:50.333
Brian: Oh, well,
this is a chiller.

00:06:50.333 --> 00:06:51.966
We very rarely use them,

00:06:51.966 --> 00:06:53.933
but it helps keep the process
water temperature

00:06:53.933 --> 00:06:55.400
in the desired temperature range

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:57.000
when it gets really hot outside,

00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.866
basically helping
the cooling tower do its job,

00:06:59.866 --> 00:07:01.366
and some of
our newer data centers,

00:07:01.366 --> 00:07:03.133
they have no chillers at all.

00:07:03.133 --> 00:07:06.400
Sandeep: I love how our new data
centers are even more efficient.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.033
By the way, can we go up and
take a look at a cooling tower?

00:07:09.033 --> 00:07:12.033
Brian: Sure.
Let's go.

00:07:13.566 --> 00:07:16.133
Sandeep: Wow,
what a view up here!

00:07:16.133 --> 00:07:18.333
Brian: So, Sandeep,
this is a cooling tower.

00:07:18.333 --> 00:07:20.866
It uses evaporation
to rapidly cool the water

00:07:20.866 --> 00:07:23.566
from the condenser loop and
sends it back down to the basin.

00:07:23.566 --> 00:07:26.366
You could say we're making
actual clouds with the Cloud.

00:07:26.366 --> 00:07:29.766
Sandeep: Clouds making actual
clouds--welcome to Google!

00:07:29.766 --> 00:07:31.966
So, Brian, how do
we power the Cloud?

00:07:31.966 --> 00:07:33.966
Brian: Well, that all starts at
Google's power substation.

00:07:33.966 --> 00:07:35.966
Let's go take a look.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.600
So this is the Google-owned
power substation.

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:43.866
This is where the high voltage
power enters the site.

00:07:43.866 --> 00:07:45.100
It's reduced and then sent

00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:46.700
to multiple power
distribution centers

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:48.000
such as this one right here.

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.800
Sandeep: What happens if
a power distribution center

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:50.900
loses power?

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:52.100
Brian: If it loses power,
we have

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:54.566
multiple generator
and utility backup sources

00:07:54.566 --> 00:07:56.866
available to maintain power
to those servers.

00:07:56.866 --> 00:07:58.566
Sandeep: And where does
all the power come from?

00:07:58.566 --> 00:07:59.633
Brian: It actually comes from

00:07:59.633 --> 00:08:01.100
multiple hydroelectric
power plants

00:08:01.100 --> 00:08:03.033
that are nearby.
Sandeep: I love how Google uses

00:08:03.033 --> 00:08:06.000
reliable green energy
whenever possible.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.066
Brian: We are
100% carbon neutral actually.

00:08:08.066 --> 00:08:09.466
Sandeep: That's pretty cool

00:08:09.466 --> 00:08:11.333
You know, it seems like
Google builds reliability

00:08:11.333 --> 00:08:13.566
from the ground up,
from the power and cooling

00:08:13.566 --> 00:08:16.366
all the way to the software
systems that manage our fleet.

00:08:16.366 --> 00:08:17.433
Thanks for showing me around,
Brian.

00:08:17.433 --> 00:08:19.466
Brian: No problem.
Have a great day.

00:08:19.466 --> 00:08:20.533
Sandeep: Thank you
for joining me on

00:08:20.533 --> 00:08:22.333
this special
behind-the-scenes tour.

00:08:22.333 --> 00:08:24.833
Please check out
cloud.google.com

00:08:24.833 --> 00:08:28.266
to learn how you can build
what's next.

